\chapter{Macros, PPX, and the extension boundary}
\label{ch:47}
\index{Macros, PPX, and the extension boundary}
\index{PPX!Unit-v1 schema}

A PPX transforms a public source AST. The host serializes the current unit, launches an authorized processor, reads a bounded response, and validates it before rebuilding source nodes. The Unit-v1 schema includes source constructs and locations that external tools may receive. It does not accept private verified HIR, internal registrations, or proofs. Normal type and ownership checking runs after rewriting.

\section{Failure modes to distinguish}
A processor can fail to start, time out, produce invalid transport data, or return structurally valid source that fails semantic checking. Preserve stderr and the compiler's wrapper diagnostic to identify the failing step. Compiler rollback of an invalid result does not undo side effects already performed by an external process. Use an identity processor to establish transport before debugging a substantial rewrite. Native authoring libraries and shipped processors are covered in \cref{ch:48}.

\section{What does an identity round trip test?}
Start with a source expression macro that expands into a function call. Add an ordinary identity processor to the compilation. At its input the call is already a source call node: the processor need not understand the macro's pattern language to return that call unchanged. On the other hand, the processor has not been given an inferred argument type on which it can safely base an overload decision. In this pipeline macro expansion precedes the first processor, whereas semantic checking of its output is still ahead.

Look at the two descriptions of the wire format. At the lowest level a symbol, a text item and a list suffice to represent trees. That tells us how to read the bytes, not whether a particular tree can occupy the expression slot of a call. A list carrying an unknown constructor could be a perfectly readable S-expression and still be invalid as a Unit-v1 expression. Such a response fails before asking whether the call's arguments type-check. In contrast, replacing one integer argument by a well-formed string literal passes the syntax question, but can make the reconstructed call ill-typed.

Use this ordering to narrow down a failure in a two-processor chain. If the first response cannot be validated, a second processor cannot legitimately repair it: validation occurs between passes. If both return valid units, inspect the final reconstructed call and the parameter it is being checked against. The latter problem is no longer explained by merely showing that the wire reader accepts lists.

